var e=JSON.parse(`[{"id":"S01","title":"The Demo Where Your Work Became His","category":"Credit and visibility","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"You spent three weeks rebuilding the checkout page's rendering pipeline. LCP dropped from 4.1s to 1.6s. In the monthly product review with the Director, your peer Karthik, who did the dashboard screenshots and two small PRs, presents the slide and says \\"we optimised the checkout flow.\\" The Director says \\"Great work, Karthik.\\" Your manager nods. You were in the room and said nothing. The review ends in five minutes.","question":"What would you do? Think about the next five minutes, the next 24 hours, and the next quarter.","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 5: So Much Depends on Reputation – Guard It with Your Life","idea":"Reputation is built from what decision-makers repeatedly see. If credit drifts once without correction it tends to drift again. Guard it calmly and early."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 9: Win Through Your Actions, Never Through Argument","idea":"Proving it with artefacts (PRs, metrics, a write-up) persuades more than arguing about who did what."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 8: Pick Your Battles Carefully – The Perfect-Economy Strategy","idea":"Don't spend capital on a public confrontation in front of the Director. Spend a little effort where it pays: the written record and the next forum."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 1: Master Your Emotional Self – The Law of Irrationality","idea":"The urge to correct him right there in the room is emotional. Let it pass and act from a cooler state."}],"sampleResponse":"In the room: if there is a natural opening, add detail without a correction tone, e.g. \\"To add to that, the main gain came from moving the price calculation off the main thread and streaming the cart. Happy to walk anyone through the profiling.\\" Detail signals ownership without accusing anyone. Within 24 hours: send a short write-up to my manager (cc the team channel, not the Director directly): what changed, before/after metrics, PR links, and what's next. Then ask my manager 1:1: \\"I want to make sure my work on checkout perf is visible for my review cycle. How do you suggest I present it to the Director next time?\\" That asks for sponsorship and doesn't complain. With Karthik, privately and neutrally: \\"Next time let's split the slide. I'll present the perf part since I'll get the deep questions.\\" Next quarter: make visibility a habit. Write demo notes, put my name on design docs, present my own work. One incident is noise. A pattern goes into a factual 1:1 with dates.","commonMistake":"Either staying silent and stewing (then venting to colleagues, which reaches Karthik and makes her look petty), or correcting him sharply in front of the Director (\\"Actually, I did that\\"), which comes across as insecure and puts the Director in an awkward spot. Both treat it as an injury to her instead of a visibility problem she can fix."},{"id":"S02","title":"The Favourite","category":"Manager dynamics","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"Your manager, Sunil, has started routing every high-visibility project to Arjun, who joined eight months ago. They go for chai together and share a hometown. You have more experience and a better delivery record. A new 'AI assistant in the admin console' initiative was just announced, the kind of project that makes someone a Lead. You hear Arjun is already 'helping scope it.'","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 19: Know Who You're Dealing With – Do Not Offend the Wrong Person","idea":"Read Sunil accurately. What does he need, what does he fear, what makes him look good to his boss? Favouritism is often about comfort and trust more than merit."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 13: When Asking for Help, Appeal to People's Self-Interest, Never to Their Mercy or Gratitude","idea":"\\"I deserve it\\" or \\"I've been loyal\\" is weak. \\"This is how I'd de-risk the launch for you\\" is strong."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 13: Know Your Enemy – The Intelligence Strategy","idea":"Here the 'enemy' is the situation, not Arjun. Learn what the initiative's success criteria are, who sponsors it, and what Sunil is being measured on."},{"source":"Arthashastra (Kautilya)","citation":"Book 1, Ch. 7 (paraphrase): sovereignty is possible only with assistance; a single wheel cannot move","idea":"Power runs through relationships. Being excellent alone without allies is a structural weakness, not a moral virtue."}],"sampleResponse":"I wouldn't compete with Arjun on chai. I'd compete on usefulness. First, I'd learn what the AI initiative needs: is it an eval harness, streaming UI, guardrails, cost control? Second, in my 1:1 I'd bring a one-page proposal: \\"I've been thinking about the admin AI assistant. Here are the three risks I see (latency, hallucinated actions, auth scoping) and how I'd handle them. I'd like to own the frontend/integration architecture. Arjun's welcome to take X.\\" That makes me the low-risk choice for Sunil's own goals. Third, I'd widen my sponsors: the PM and the skip-level should see my work through demos and docs, so my career doesn't depend on one person's comfort. If after one or two cycles the pattern continues despite clear asks, I'd treat it as data about this team (useful for my job search), not as a verdict on me.","commonMistake":"Complaining about 'unfairness' to HR or peers, becoming cold with Arjun, or quietly working harder and hoping someone notices. Effort without positioning rarely gets noticed, and visible resentment makes the manager more comfortable with the favourite."},{"id":"S03","title":"Talked Over in the Design Review","category":"Meetings and presence","difficulty":"easy","scenario":"In architecture reviews, Vikram, a senior backend engineer, regularly starts talking while you're mid-sentence. Today you were explaining why the React Server Components migration should be phased. He cut in with \\"Yeah but basically we just need to move fast,\\" and the discussion moved on. Two people looked at you.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 1: Master Your Emotional Self – The Law of Irrationality","idea":"The sting is real, but a flustered reaction makes her look less senior than him. Composure is the asset here."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 3: Amidst the Turmoil of Events, Do Not Lose Your Presence of Mind – The Counterbalance Strategy","idea":"Under pressure, slow down. A calm, deliberate voice reclaims the floor better than a louder one."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 4: Always Say Less Than Necessary","idea":"Short, structured statements are harder to interrupt and carry more weight than long explanations."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 34: Be Royal in Your Own Fashion: Act Like a King to Be Treated Like One","idea":"People treat you the way you carry yourself. Claim the floor as if it's normal that you have it."}],"sampleResponse":"In the moment: hold up a hand slightly and say evenly, \\"Let me finish this point, it's relevant to speed,\\" then make it in two sentences: \\"Phasing is the fast option. A big-bang migration means one bad hydration bug blocks every release.\\" Then turn it back: \\"Vikram, what's your concern with phasing?\\" That shows I'm not rattled and pulls him into my framing. Before the next review: send a short pre-read so my position is on record before anyone can talk over it. Ask the facilitator to go round the room on key decisions. If it keeps happening, a private, non-accusatory word with Vikram: \\"I notice we keep cutting across each other in reviews. I want to hear your points fully, and I'd like to finish mine.\\"","commonMistake":"Going quiet for the rest of the meeting and replaying it all evening, or snapping (\\"Can you please let me talk!\\") in a way that makes her look emotional. Another trap is assuming it's personal hostility when it may just be his habit with everyone. Find out before escalating."},{"id":"S04","title":"The Peer Who Undermines in Small Ways","category":"Peer conflict","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"Divya, a peer at your level, leaves condescending code review comments on your PRs (\\"Not sure this was thought through?\\"), mentions in standup that \\"the frontend is always the bottleneck,\\" and in a Slack thread with your manager wrote that your component library \\"has a lot of tech debt.\\" None of it is quite outrageous, but over time it adds up to a narrative.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 16: See the Hostility Behind the Friendly Facade – The Law of Aggression","idea":"Chronic small digs are often a covert form of aggression or competition. Recognise the pattern early, before the narrative hardens."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 25: Occupy the Moral High Ground – The Righteous Strategy","idea":"Stay the visibly fair, constructive party. Being right counts for less than being seen as reasonable."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 18: Do Not Build Fortresses to Protect Yourself – Isolation Is Dangerous","idea":"Withdrawing and getting defensive feeds her story. Engaging and building relationships around her starves it."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 5: So Much Depends on Reputation – Guard It with Your Life","idea":"A narrative repeated without a counter-narrative becomes 'what everyone knows.' Counter it with facts, not complaints."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd run it on three tracks. (1) Neutralise the substance: in the Slack thread, reply calmly with facts: \\"Fair point on tech debt. Here's the current list, the two items we're fixing this sprint, and the trade-off we made in Q2.\\" Answering openly makes the jab look like an agenda item I own. (2) Code reviews: respond to the technical point only, and ask for specifics: \\"Which part concerns you? Happy to pair on it.\\" Vague digs either become concrete or stop. (3) Private conversation: \\"Divya, I've noticed some friction between us in reviews and standup. If something about how frontend works is blocking you, I'd rather hear it directly.\\" That gives her an exit and signals I've noticed. Separately, I'd keep a private log of dates and examples. I wouldn't use it yet, but it's there if this escalates to my manager. And I'd keep strengthening relationships with the backend leads so her 'frontend is the bottleneck' story doesn't match what they see.","commonMistake":"Answering in kind with digs, or complaining to the manager with feelings rather than facts (\\"She's always so negative to me\\"), which looks like a two-person squabble. Or ignoring it completely until the narrative shows up in her performance review."},{"id":"S05","title":"Just One More Small Thing","category":"Scope and boundaries","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"Two days before the sprint ends, the PM, Neha, pings: \\"Small thing: can we also add CSV export to the analytics page? Leadership wants it for Thursday.\\" Last sprint it was 'small' i18n support; before that, 'just' dark mode. Each time you absorbed it with late nights. Your planned work, a11y fixes for an enterprise customer, is now at risk.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 29: Take Small Bites – The Fait Accompli Strategy","idea":"Recognise the pattern being used on her: each small ask seems too minor to refuse, and together they change the deal. Name the pattern rather than fight each bite."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 31: Control the Options: Get Others to Play with the Cards You Deal","idea":"Don't give a yes or no. Give choices that all protect the important work: \\"A or B, which do you prefer?\\""},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 29: Plan All the Way to the End","idea":"Think past Thursday. Where does always absorbing scope lead? Burnout, missed commitments, and a reputation as the one who'll always stretch."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd reply quickly and make the trade-off visible: \\"Happy to help get leadership what they need. Right now Thursday has the a11y fixes for Acme, which are tied to their renewal. Options: (A) CSV export by Thursday, a11y slips to next Tuesday, and I'll let the account team know; (B) a11y on Thursday, CSV by Monday; (C) a quick 'copy table to clipboard' by Thursday as a stopgap, proper CSV next sprint. Which works?\\" That lets her own the priority call. Then in retro or a 1:1 I'd raise the pattern without blame: \\"Three sprints in a row we've added work mid-sprint. Can we agree that mid-sprint asks come with a swap?\\" I'd mention it lightly to my manager so they're not surprised if something slips.","commonMistake":"Saying yes again and working late to prove commitment, then resenting Neha, or flatly saying no (\\"That's not in scope\\"), which makes her the reasonable one in leadership's eyes. Both miss the move: make the trade-off visible and let the requester choose."},{"id":"S06","title":"The Mandatory Weekend","category":"Boundaries","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"Friday 6 pm: your Director announces in the team channel that \\"everyone is expected online Saturday and Sunday\\" to hit a launch date set by sales. You had a family function planned on Sunday, booked weeks ago. Two colleagues have already replied \\"Sure 👍\\". This is the third 'all-hands weekend' this quarter.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 14: Resist the Downward Pull of the Group – The Law of Conformity","idea":"The quick \\"Sure 👍\\" replies are group pressure at work. Pause and decide from her own priorities, not the chat's momentum."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 8: Pick Your Battles Carefully – The Perfect-Economy Strategy","idea":"Refusing the whole weekend is a big battle. Negotiating part of it costs little and keeps goodwill."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 31: Control the Options: Get Others to Play with the Cards You Deal","idea":"Offer a concrete plan instead of a yes or no."}],"sampleResponse":"I wouldn't reply in the group thread. I'd DM my manager: \\"I'm committed to the launch. I have a family function Sunday that was booked weeks ago. Plan: I'll be fully online Saturday and take the checkout and feature-flag items, and Sunday I'll be reachable on phone for urgent issues from 6 pm. I can also hand off X to Ravi. Does that work?\\" I'd deliver strongly on Saturday so the trade looks generous, not reluctant. Separately, since it's the third time, I'd raise it at the next 1:1 as a planning question, not a grievance: \\"What's driving these launch dates? Can engineering be in the loop before sales commits?\\" Weekend crunch every month is also information about this company's culture. I'd note it for my job search.","commonMistake":"Either quietly cancelling the family function and resenting it (setting a precedent that her weekends are free), or posting a public refusal or a sarcastic comment in the channel. The emotional version: deciding \\"this company doesn't respect me\\" instead of negotiating a specific deal."},{"id":"S07","title":"Asking for the Raise","category":"Negotiation","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"Appraisal season. You led the migration to a new design system used by 14 teams and mentored two juniors. You've learned that a recent external hire at your level earns about 30% more than you. Your manager says, \\"Budgets are tight this year, everyone's getting around 8%.\\" You have a 1:1 with him tomorrow.","question":"What would you do? What is your ask, how do you frame it, and what do you do if he says no?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 13: When Asking for Help, Appeal to People's Self-Interest, Never to Their Mercy or Gratitude","idea":"Frame the raise around what it does for him and the org: retention of a key person, a cheaper option than replacing her, leverage on the design system."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 21: Negotiate While Advancing – The Diplomatic-War Strategy","idea":"Keep strengthening your position while you talk: visible impact, market data, and real outside options."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 35: Master the Art of Timing","idea":"Budgets are set before the formal appraisal conversation. Ask early, and ask before or right after a visible win."},{"source":"Chanakya Niti","citation":"Ch. 2, v. 7 (paraphrase of the common English translation): do not reveal what you plan to do; keep it to yourself and carry it out","idea":"Don't threaten to leave or reveal her job search as a bluff. Let her outside options support her calm, not appear as an ultimatum."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd go in with a document, not a feeling: (1) impact: design system adopted by 14 teams, estimated X engineer-weeks saved, two juniors now shipping independently; (2) scope: I'm doing Lead-level work; (3) market: \\"From market conversations, Lead/Senior FE roles in Hyderabad product companies are in the ₹X–Y range.\\" Then the ask: \\"I'd like my compensation aligned to that, a correction of about 25–30%, or a path to Lead this cycle with the adjustment.\\" If he says budget is fixed: \\"I understand the pool is tight. What can we do outside the merit pool: a market correction, a retention bonus, or promotion? What would you need from me to make that case to your manager?\\" Then I'd get a written next step and date. If it's still a no, I'd accept it calmly and quietly speed up my search. The no tells me what this company thinks I'm worth.","commonMistake":"Bringing up the colleague's salary as an injustice (\\"It's not fair that he earns more\\"), getting emotional, or threatening to quit without being ready to. The other common mistake is not asking at all and accepting 8% while silently resenting it."},{"id":"S08","title":"The Offer Call","category":"Negotiation","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"A Bengaluru-headquartered AI product company (with a Hyderabad office) wants to hire you as Lead Full Stack. The recruiter calls: \\"We're excited! Quick question, what's your current CTC and expected? We need it to proceed.\\" You're in final rounds with one other company. Your current CTC is below market.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 4: Always Say Less Than Necessary","idea":"The first number named tends to anchor the negotiation. Volunteering a low current CTC anchors her low."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 8: Make Other People Come to You – Use Bait If Necessary","idea":"Once they want her, let them make the first concrete move. Her value goes up when she isn't chasing."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 21: Negotiate While Advancing – The Diplomatic-War Strategy","idea":"Keep the other process moving. Real alternatives are the strongest position in any negotiation."},{"source":"Chanakya Niti","citation":"Ch. 2, v. 7 (paraphrase): keep your plans to yourself until you carry them out","idea":"Share facts that help her, keep strategic details (exact other offer, deadlines) close until they're useful."}],"sampleResponse":"\\"I'm excited about this role too. I'd prefer to focus on the value of this role rather than my current package, since I'm moving into a Lead scope. What's the budgeted range for this position?\\" If pushed for a number: \\"Based on the scope and market data for Lead Full Stack/AI roles, I'm looking in the ₹X–Y range for fixed, and I'm open on structure: joining bonus, ESOPs.\\" (Y should be ambitious but defensible.) If they insist on current CTC for HR forms, I'd give it accurately, because lying burns reputation, but immediately say it isn't the reference point. I'd mention I'm \\"in final stages with another company\\" only when it's true, without bluffing details. When the offer comes: thank them, ask for it in writing, take 24–48 hours, and counter on one or two specific items with reasons.","commonMistake":"Blurting out current CTC and a modest hike (\\"30% would be good\\"), or inventing a fake competing offer, which can blow up if checked. Emotionally: feeling grateful just to be wanted and accepting the first number to avoid seeming greedy."},{"id":"S09","title":"\\"Tell Us About Your Manager\\"","category":"Interviews","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"Final round, hiring manager interview. Friendly tone, then: \\"Why are you leaving? Honestly, what's the biggest problem with your current manager or team?\\" The truthful answer involves a manager who plays favourites and constant weekend crunch.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 5: So Much Depends on Reputation – Guard It with Your Life","idea":"The interviewer is picturing how she'll talk about them one day. Criticising a current employer reads as risk."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 3: See Through People's Masks – The Law of Role-playing","idea":"A friendly, 'off the record' tone is part of the interview. Treat it as being on stage."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 4: Always Say Less Than Necessary","idea":"Say a short, true answer that points forward, not a detailed backstory."}],"sampleResponse":"\\"I've learned a lot there. I took the design system from zero to 14 teams. What I'm looking for now is a Lead scope on an AI-first product, where I can own architecture end to end and the product moves faster. In a large company, those roles are fewer and tied to longer cycles.\\" If pushed on the manager: \\"Like any team, we have different styles. I've learned to be explicit about priorities and to make my work visible, which I think is a Lead skill anyway.\\" Everything I said is true, it's forward-looking, and it turns the question into evidence of maturity.","commonMistake":"Taking the bait because the interviewer seems sympathetic and venting about favouritism and weekends. Even when it's accurate, it marks her as someone who complains. The opposite mistake is a too-polished non-answer that sounds evasive. Give one real, forward-looking reason."},{"id":"S10","title":"The Stress-Test Interviewer","category":"Interviews","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"In a system design round for an AI full-stack role, you propose streaming LLM responses with SSE and a token-level UI. The interviewer interrupts: \\"That's naive. Nobody does it that way at scale. Why would you?\\" He folds his arms and waits. You're fairly sure your approach is reasonable.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 3: Amidst the Turmoil of Events, Do Not Lose Your Presence of Mind – The Counterbalance Strategy","idea":"Pushback is often a deliberate test of composure. The calm one controls the room."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 7: Soften People's Resistance by Confirming Their Self-opinion – The Law of Defensiveness","idea":"Acknowledging his expertise and engaging with his concern gets more cooperation than arguing back."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 9: Win Through Your Actions, Never Through Argument","idea":"Show the reasoning and trade-offs instead of insisting you're right."}],"sampleResponse":"Pause, then: \\"Good challenge. Let me pressure-test it. SSE's main costs at scale are long-lived connections, proxy and load-balancer timeouts, and reconnect handling. Options: SSE behind a gateway tuned for streaming, WebSockets if we need bidirectional events like tool-call confirmations, or plain polling for low-priority jobs. For a chat-style assistant with mostly server-to-client tokens, I'd still pick SSE, with resumable event IDs and backpressure. What scale or constraint were you thinking of? That might change my choice.\\" I stay curious and specific, and I either defend with reasons or update gracefully if he brings a new constraint.","commonMistake":"Caving immediately (\\"You're right, sorry, I'll use WebSockets\\") without reasoning, which reads as having no conviction, or getting defensive and taking it personally. Both treat the pushback as an attack rather than as a test."},{"id":"S11","title":"Reading the New Team's Power Map","category":"Power mapping","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"You've transferred to a new 'GenAI Platform' team. The org chart says the EM, Priya, runs it. But in your first two weeks you notice the staff engineer, Rohan, effectively decides architecture. A PM from the US office gets her way in every priority debate. One quiet senior, Lakshmi, seems to be the person everyone DMs when stuck.","question":"What would you do? What do you do in your first 30 days?","principles":[{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 13: Know Your Enemy – The Intelligence Strategy","idea":"The principle is to gather intelligence before acting. In a new team, the 'opponent' is your own ignorance of how things actually work."},{"source":"Arthashastra (Kautilya)","citation":"Book 6 (Mandala theory, paraphrase): see the 'circle of states' as allies, rivals, neutrals and middle powers around you","idea":"Map relationships, not just titles. Who allies with whom, who is neutral, who is the go-between."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 38: Think As You Like but Behave Like Others","idea":"Early on, respect the team's norms and rituals even if you'd change them. Earn standing before trying to change things."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 19: Know Who You're Dealing With – Do Not Offend the Wrong Person","idea":"The person with informal power (Rohan, the US PM) can block you quietly. Don't accidentally challenge them in week two."}],"sampleResponse":"Days 1–10: listen. 1:1s with everyone, asking, \\"What's working, what's frustrating, who should I learn from?\\" I'd note who gets mentioned repeatedly (likely Lakshmi). I'd read past design docs to see who actually approved decisions. Days 10–20: build trust with each power centre in its own currency. With Rohan, ask for his view on my first design before the review, so he's a co-author, not a judge. With the US PM, overlap her hours once a week and understand her customer pressures. With Lakshmi, help her with something she's stuck on. With Priya, the formal boss, give clear updates and no surprises, and never route around her. Days 20–30: ship one visible, useful thing that fits the team's priorities, ideally co-owned with someone influential. Throughout, I'd keep my private map to myself and not gossip about who 'really' runs things.","commonMistake":"Trying to impress by challenging the architecture in week one, aligning only with the formal manager while ignoring informal power, or reading informal power as 'politics' and refusing to engage. Saying out loud that 'Rohan really runs this team' is also a mistake."},{"id":"S12","title":"The Colleague Who Overshares","category":"Gossip and discretion","difficulty":"easy","scenario":"Over lunch, Meera, a friendly colleague, tells you in detail that Sunil (your manager) is 'on thin ice' with the Director, that Arjun got a special retention bonus, and that someone in QA is about to be put on a PIP. Then she leans in: \\"So what's your take on Sunil? You must have stories.\\"","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"Chanakya Niti","citation":"Ch. 2, v. 6 (paraphrase of common translation): do not fully trust even an ordinary friend, for if angered they may reveal your secrets","idea":"Someone who shares others' secrets freely will share yours. Treat what you say to her as public."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 4: Always Say Less Than Necessary","idea":"The less she says, the less can be quoted, twisted or traced back to her."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 5: So Much Depends on Reputation – Guard It with Your Life","idea":"Being known as discreet is a long-term asset, especially for someone aiming at Lead, where people bring you sensitive things."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd listen politely and take it as unverified information, not fact. When asked: \\"Honestly, Sunil and I are fine. We're focused on the Q4 launch. How's your migration going?\\" I'd give a warm deflection and change the subject, with no lecture. I wouldn't repeat what she told me. Privately, I'd note what might be useful if true (e.g., a reorg possibility) and quietly make sure my work is visible to more than one leader, just in case. I'd stay friendly with Meera, because she's a good source of the team's mood, but I'd never confide in her.","commonMistake":"Joining in to bond (\\"Oh, don't get me started on Sunil...\\"). That's exactly the line that will be repeated. Or the opposite: moralising to Meera (\\"We shouldn't gossip\\"), which turns a harmless contact into an enemy."},{"id":"S13","title":"The Anxious Manager Before the Exec Review","category":"Managing up","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"Your manager, nervous about a VP review next week, is micromanaging: asking for hourly updates, rewriting your slides, and second-guessing the technical approach you'd already agreed on. You're losing time and feeling distrusted.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 1: Never Outshine the Master","idea":"His anxiety is about how he'll look to the VP. Make him look competent rather than showing you're the smarter one."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 2: Transform Self-love into Empathy – The Law of Narcissism","idea":"Look past the irritation to what he's feeling (exposure, fear). Empathy gives her a way to influence him."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 7: Soften People's Resistance by Confirming Their Self-opinion – The Law of Defensiveness","idea":"He'll loosen up if his view of himself as a competent leader is confirmed, not challenged."}],"sampleResponse":"Reframe: this isn't distrust of me. He's afraid of the VP. So I'd reduce his fear proactively. I'd send a short daily status at a fixed time (done / risks / decisions needed) so he stops polling. I'd offer: \\"Want to do a 20-minute dry run of the VP deck Thursday? I'll prep likely questions and answers for you.\\" On the slides: \\"I took your edits. I kept the architecture slide as-is because the VP's team asked about latency last time. Happy to go through why.\\" I'd let him present the headline and offer to handle the deep technical questions. After the review goes well, I'd ask: \\"That went well. Can we go back to weekly check-ins for the build phase?\\" Now I've earned the autonomy.","commonMistake":"Taking it personally (\\"He doesn't trust me\\"), pushing back defensively, or showing in the review that she understands the system better than he does. That embarrasses him and makes things worse for months."},{"id":"S14","title":"When Your Peer Starts to Resent You","category":"Envy","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"After your LLM-powered search prototype got praised in the all-hands, a peer you were friendly with, Sneha, has gone cold. She makes jokes like \\"Oh, here comes the AI star,\\" stopped inviting you to the team lunch group, and you hear she told someone you're \\"good at self-promotion.\\"","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 10: Beware the Fragile Ego – The Law of Envy","idea":"Envy rarely announces itself. It comes as jokes, coldness and 'concerns' about character. Recognise it so you don't take the bait or feed it."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 46: Never Appear Too Perfect","idea":"Visible success attracts envy. Showing a little vulnerability and sharing credit lowers the threat she represents."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 26: Deny Them Targets – The Strategy of the Void","idea":"Don't give envy material: no boasting, no retaliating, no drama she can point to."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd recognise this as envy, not a verdict on me, and lower the temperature. Publicly: share credit generously (\\"Sneha's feedback on the search UX helped\\" if true, and it often is) and mention what was hard or failed in the prototype. That shows struggle, not just shine. Privately: an honest, warm check-in without accusation: \\"I feel like things got a bit weird between us after the all-hands. I value working with you. Is something bothering you?\\" Then I'd look for ways to give her visibility, e.g., invite her to co-own the next phase of a piece that matches her strengths. I'd make sure my self-promotion is factual and team-oriented so the 'self-promoter' label doesn't stick. If she continues undermining after that, I'd keep my distance, document if needed, and not confide in her.","commonMistake":"Shrinking herself, playing down good work to be liked, or getting hurt and defensive (\\"I don't know why she's so jealous\\") in a way that spreads. Also: boasting more to 'prove' herself, which confirms the label."},{"id":"S15","title":"The Quiet Job Search","category":"Career moves","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"You're interviewing for Lead roles. A colleague, Ravi, messages: \\"Saw you at the Hyderabad AI meetup with recruiters from Razorpay and Sarvam 😄 Planning something?\\" Your manager is also suddenly asking, \\"How are you feeling about your growth here?\\"","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 3: Conceal Your Intentions","idea":"Until an offer is signed, an open job search can cost her: being left out of key projects, earlier exits, a weaker negotiating position."},{"source":"Chanakya Niti","citation":"Ch. 2, v. 7 (paraphrase): do not reveal what you plan to do; keep it to yourself and carry it out","idea":"Keep plans private until they're done."},{"source":"Chanakya Niti","citation":"Ch. 1, v. 8 (paraphrase of common translation): do not live where you are not respected, cannot earn a living, have no friends, or cannot gain knowledge","idea":"Leaving a place that no longer helps her grow is legitimate. It's practical judgement, not disloyalty."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 5: So Much Depends on Reputation – Guard It with Your Life","idea":"Lying outright is risky if found out. Deflect truthfully instead of denying."}],"sampleResponse":"To Ravi: \\"Ha, I go to those for the talks. The agent-evals session was really good. You should come next time.\\" It's true and it gives nothing away. To my manager, a sincere but non-revealing answer: \\"I want to grow into a Lead role, ideally with more AI/LLM scope. What would that look like here in the next 6–12 months?\\" That's honest, gives him the chance to offer something, and doesn't reveal the search. Operationally: interview on personal time and devices, no company laptop, no 'Open to Work' badge visible to recruiters at my company, and keep delivering well so I leave with my reputation intact. When I resign, I do it gracefully and with good handover. The industry in Hyderabad is small.","commonMistake":"Telling a few 'trusted' colleagues and assuming it won't spread, making flat denials that later look dishonest, or slacking off because she's 'leaving anyway.' The emotional version: feeling guilty, as if looking is a betrayal."},{"id":"S16","title":"The Skip-Level Asks for 'Honest Feedback'","category":"Managing up","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"Your skip-level, the Director, schedules a 'casual coffee' and asks: \\"I'm hearing mixed things about Sunil's team. You can be honest with me. How is he as a manager?\\" You do have real concerns: favouritism and poor planning.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 19: Know Who You're Dealing With – Do Not Offend the Wrong Person","idea":"Why is he asking? Is he building a case, checking a rumour, or testing her discretion? Her words may travel to Sunil."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 3: See Through People's Masks – The Law of Role-playing","idea":"'Casual' and 'you can be honest' is the framing of the conversation, not a guarantee of safety."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 4: Always Say Less Than Necessary","idea":"Specific, measured and factual is better than a full download."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 5: So Much Depends on Reputation – Guard It with Your Life","idea":"She wants to come across as constructive and fair. Someone who criticises a manager readily will one day criticise the Director too."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd be honest but structured, and talk about systems, not character: \\"Sunil cares about delivery and he's been supportive on X. The two things that would help the team most: earlier involvement in planning, since we've had three crunch weekends this quarter driven by dates set before eng sized them, and clearer criteria for how high-visibility projects are assigned, so people can see a path.\\" Everything is verifiable and fixable, and none of it is gossip. I'd ask: \\"Is there something specific you're trying to understand?\\" That reads his intent. I wouldn't name colleagues or share others' complaints. I'd assume Sunil may hear a version of this, and I'd be comfortable if he did.","commonMistake":"Unloading every frustration because the Director seems to be on her side, or going fully loyal (\\"Everything's great\\") and missing a real chance to improve things. Both are emotional reactions, either grievance or fear, rather than a measured read."},{"id":"S17","title":"The Glue-Work Trap","category":"Positioning","difficulty":"easy","scenario":"You've become the person who organises sprint demos, writes meeting notes, onboards new joiners and plans team outings. People thank you warmly. But in your appraisal, your manager says, \\"You're great for the team, but we need to see more technical leadership.\\" Meanwhile, others got the architecture work.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 25: Re-create Yourself","idea":"How others see you is partly something you author. If the team sees her as the 'organiser,' she has to deliberately redefine her role."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 13: Advance with a Sense of Purpose – The Law of Aimlessness","idea":"Without a clear direction, she gets pulled toward whatever others need. Decide what she's building toward (Lead / AI full-stack) and filter work through it."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 8: Pick Your Battles Carefully – The Perfect-Economy Strategy","idea":"Spend her limited time and energy where it compounds toward her goal."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd treat the feedback as useful data and act on it. First, rotate the glue work: \\"Let's rotate demo-hosting and notes weekly. I'll make a schedule.\\" That's a fair system, not a refusal. Second, ask explicitly for a technical stretch: \\"I want to own the architecture for the next feature, e.g. the RAG-based help widget. What would you need to see from me to trust me with it?\\" Third, make my technical work visible: write the design doc, run the review, present at the engineering guild. Keep the parts of glue work that are leadership (mentoring, onboarding), and frame them that way in reviews: \\"Onboarded three engineers, cutting ramp-up from 6 to 3 weeks.\\"","commonMistake":"Continuing to say yes to everything because it feels good to be needed, then feeling unappreciated, or abruptly refusing all team tasks in a way that seems sulky. Neither changes how others see her."},{"id":"S18","title":"Inheriting the Departed Lead's Project","category":"Leadership","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"The well-liked Tech Lead, Anand, left suddenly. You've been asked to take over his half-built AI chatbot project, which is late, poorly tested and has an over-promised demo to a big client in four weeks. The team is loyal to Anand's way of doing things and keeps saying, \\"Anand would have done it like this.\\"","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 41: Avoid Stepping into a Great Man's Shoes","idea":"Trying to be 'the new Anand' invites unfavourable comparison. Set out her own approach and make her own mark."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 45: Preach the Need for Change, but Never Reform Too Much at Once","idea":"Change things in stages and respect the team's attachments, or they'll resist."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 29: Plan All the Way to the End","idea":"Plan backwards from the client demo: what must be true in four weeks, and what has to be cut."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 12: Lose Battles but Win the War – Grand Strategy","idea":"Accept small losses (cutting features, resetting expectations) to get the bigger win: a credible launch and her own credibility."}],"sampleResponse":"Week 1: assess and reset. I'd audit the code, tests and client promises, then go to my manager with an honest status: \\"Here's what's realistic in four weeks: core Q&A flow with guardrails and evals. Here's what we should move to phase 2.\\" Better to reset expectations now than fail in front of the client. With the team: acknowledge Anand's work publicly (\\"Anand set up a solid retrieval layer\\") and keep what's good, but make it clear we're moving forward: \\"Let's keep what works, and here are two changes I want: eval tests before merge and a demo-freeze date.\\" Only two changes, not ten. I'd give the most loyal team member ownership of a key piece so they become a stakeholder in the new approach. After the demo, I'd introduce further changes from a position of earned trust.","commonMistake":"Either trying to imitate Anand to keep everyone comfortable, or changing everything in week one to assert authority. Also: promising to deliver the full over-promised scope out of fear of looking weak, then failing in front of the client."},{"id":"S19","title":"Pitching AI Over the Architect's Objection","category":"Influence","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"You want to introduce an LLM-based feature (auto-summaries for support tickets) into the product. The principal architect, Mr. Rao, 20 years in, dismisses it in a review: \\"AI is a fad, it hallucinates, we're not going there.\\" Several juniors privately agree with you, but nobody speaks up.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 7: Soften People's Resistance by Confirming Their Self-opinion – The Law of Defensiveness","idea":"Rao sees himself as the guardian of quality. Frame the proposal as serving that, so he isn't defending against her."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 45: Preach the Need for Change, but Never Reform Too Much at Once","idea":"A small, reversible pilot is easier to accept than a 'platform shift'."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 9: Win Through Your Actions, Never Through Argument","idea":"A working prototype with eval results beats any debate about whether AI is a fad."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 29: Take Small Bites – The Fait Accompli Strategy","idea":"Used ethically and openly: progress in small, approved steps (internal tool, then opt-in beta, then default) rather than one big ask."}],"sampleResponse":"I wouldn't argue in the meeting. I'd say, \\"Hallucination is the right concern. Can I come back with data?\\" Then I'd go to Rao privately: \\"Your point on hallucination stuck with me. What would you need to see to consider a controlled pilot?\\" He sets the bar, so he owns it. I'd build a small internal-only prototype for support agents (human in the loop, no customer exposure), with an eval set of 200 real tickets, accuracy metrics and failure cases. I'd present it with his criteria up front: \\"Mr. Rao asked for X. Here's how it did.\\" If it works, he gets credit for the rigour. If it fails, I've learned cheaply and lost no standing.","commonMistake":"Pushing back hard in the meeting, or going around him to the VP, which makes a permanent enemy of someone influential. Or giving up entirely and complaining that 'seniors block innovation.'"},{"id":"S20","title":"The Postmortem Blame Game","category":"Self-protection","difficulty":"hard","scenario":"A release broke payments for 40 minutes. In the postmortem doc, the backend lead has written: \\"Root cause: frontend change in checkout.\\" The truth: your frontend change exposed a backend API that returned a malformed response under a race condition the backend team had known about. Your manager forwarded the doc to you with \\"??\\"","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 3: Amidst the Turmoil of Events, Do Not Lose Your Presence of Mind – The Counterbalance Strategy","idea":"Respond with evidence and calm, not defensiveness, especially in a high-stakes document."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 5: So Much Depends on Reputation – Guard It with Your Life","idea":"Postmortem docs are read by leadership and stay on record. An inaccurate root cause needs correcting, through facts."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 26: Keep Your Hands Clean","idea":"Read as self-protection: recognise when you're being set up as the scapegoat, and refuse that role cleanly."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 25: Occupy the Moral High Ground – The Righteous Strategy","idea":"Champion a blameless, systems-focused postmortem. That's correct and also hard to argue against."}],"sampleResponse":"First, gather facts: timeline, logs, the API response, the existing backend ticket about the race condition. Then reply to my manager: \\"Looking into it. Early read: the frontend change triggered it, but the root cause looks like the known race in /payment-intent (ticket #1234). Sharing a timeline in an hour.\\" In the doc, a comment, not an edit war: \\"Suggest we separate trigger from root cause. Trigger: checkout change at 14:02. Root cause: malformed response under concurrent requests (logs attached; known in #1234). Contributing factor: no contract test between FE and API. I'll own adding contract tests on our side.\\" Owning my part (no contract test) makes me credible, and the facts shift the root cause without any accusation. Propose actions for both teams.","commonMistake":"Either accepting the blame quietly to avoid conflict, which lets the record stand and hurts her reputation, or firing back angrily (\\"This is completely wrong, backend is blaming us!\\") and turning a technical record into a team war."},{"id":"S21","title":"The Friend Who Needs a 'Small' Loan","category":"Friends and favours","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"A college friend, Pallavi, calls. She's starting a cloud-kitchen business and asks you to lend ₹3 lakh, \\"just for six months.\\" She previously borrowed ₹40,000 and repaid it late after reminders. She adds, \\"I'd do the same for you. We're like sisters.\\"","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"Chanakya Niti","citation":"Ch. 1, v. 11 (common English translation): test a servant while in the discharge of his duty, a relative in difficulty, a friend in adversity, and a wife in misfortune","idea":"Character shows in hard times and in how past obligations were honoured. Her late repayment is the real data."},{"source":"Chanakya Niti","citation":"Ch. 1, v. 7 (paraphrase): save your wealth against future calamity","idea":"Protecting her financial buffer is wisdom, not selfishness, especially with a job switch coming."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 2: Never Put Too Much Trust in Friends, Learn How to Use Enemies","idea":"Read narrowly: money and friendship mix badly, because friends feel entitled to leniency. Set terms as you would with anyone."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 4: Determine the Strength of People's Character – The Law of Compulsive Behavior","idea":"People tend to repeat patterns. Judge by past actions more than present promises."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd separate the friendship from the loan and decide with a clear head. If I'm not comfortable: \\"Pallavi, I'm really happy you're starting this. I have a policy of not lending large amounts. I'm saving for a move this year. What I can do is help with your ordering website or connect you with someone who does small business loans.\\" That's warm, firm, and it offers real help without the money. If I do want to help, I'd only give an amount I could afford to lose and would think of as a gift, or put it in writing with a repayment schedule. No 'sisters' exception.","commonMistake":"Saying yes out of guilt or the emotional appeal (\\"We're like sisters\\"), then resenting her when repayment is late again, or over-justifying the no in a way that invites bargaining. Lending what she can't afford to lose puts both the money and the friendship at risk."},{"id":"S22","title":"Family Pressure to Stay 'Safe'","category":"Family and society","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"Over Sunday lunch, your parents and an aunt push back on your plan to leave your MNC for an AI startup: \\"Why leave a big brand company? Startups shut down. What will people say if it fails? Also, it's time to think about settling down.\\" You love them and want their blessing, but you've made your decision.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 13: Advance with a Sense of Purpose – The Law of Aimlessness","idea":"A clear sense of purpose lets her stay steady under social pressure without needing to win every argument."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 9: Win Through Your Actions, Never Through Argument","idea":"Arguing at lunch hardens positions. Results over time will convince them."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 11: Trade Space for Time – The Nonengagement Strategy","idea":"She doesn't need their approval today. Give ground in the conversation while holding firm on the decision."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 7: Soften People's Resistance by Confirming Their Self-opinion – The Law of Defensiveness","idea":"They see themselves as protective, loving parents. Affirm that, and they relax."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd address their fear, not their argument: \\"I know you're saying this because you care about my security, and I'm grateful. Let me tell you how I've thought about the risk: I'm choosing a well-funded company, I'll have six months' savings as a buffer, and AI engineering skills are in demand. Even if this company fails, I'm more employable, not less.\\" I'd show them the plan, then not litigate it. On marriage: \\"That's important to me too, and I'll decide in my own time. Today I wanted to share the job news.\\" A calm boundary, then a change of subject. Over the next months, I'd share small wins so the decision proves itself.","commonMistake":"Getting into a heated argument and leaving upset, or caving to keep the peace. A quieter version: needing their approval to feel the decision is valid, and so re-arguing it at every family gathering."},{"id":"S23","title":"The Landlord's 15% Hike","category":"Negotiation (non-office)","difficulty":"medium","scenario":"Your Gachibowli landlord messages: rent goes up 15% from next month, or \\"many people are waiting for this flat.\\" You've been a good tenant for three years: always on time, no damage, you even fixed the geyser yourself. You'd prefer to stay, but similar flats nearby are about 5–8% higher than what you pay now.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 13: When Asking for Help, Appeal to People's Self-Interest, Never to Their Mercy or Gratitude","idea":"Not \\"please, I've been good\\" but \\"keeping a reliable tenant saves you vacancy, brokerage and risk.\\""},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 21: Negotiate While Advancing – The Diplomatic-War Strategy","idea":"Have real alternatives (shortlisted flats) before negotiating. Her calm comes from options, not bluff."},{"source":"Arthashastra (Kautilya)","citation":"The four upayas: sama (conciliation), dana (gifts/concessions), bheda (division), danda (force) (paraphrase; widely attributed to the Arthashastra tradition)","idea":"Use the gentle, legitimate means first: rapport and a fair concession. Here, force or creating division is neither needed nor appropriate."}],"sampleResponse":"Before replying, I'd check 3–4 comparable listings and maybe visit one, so I have a real fallback. Then: \\"Uncle, I've been happy here for three years and would like to stay. I've always paid on time and maintained the flat. Comparable flats in this area are going for about ₹X, roughly 6% above current rent. I can do 6% and sign a two-year agreement, so you avoid vacancy, brokerage and painting costs.\\" If he holds at 15%, I'd offer a middle point (8–9%) with the longer lock-in, or walk away politely if the numbers don't make sense. Moving is a cost, but so is overpaying every month.","commonMistake":"Either accepting immediately out of fear of moving, or reacting emotionally (\\"After everything I've done for this flat!\\"). Appealing to gratitude puts her at the landlord's mercy instead of making a business case."},{"id":"S24","title":"The Interior Vendor Who Keeps Slipping","category":"Negotiation (non-office)","difficulty":"easy","scenario":"You paid a 50% advance to a modular-kitchen vendor. The install was due three weeks ago. Each call gets \\"next week, madam, pakka.\\" You've noticed he responds faster to your male neighbour, who has the same vendor. The remaining 50% is still with you.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 10: Create a Threatening Presence – Deterrence Strategies","idea":"Make delay costlier for him than delivery, through legitimate means: written timelines, payment held until milestones, reviews."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 4: Always Say Less Than Necessary","idea":"Long, emotional calls are easy to brush off. Short written messages with dates and consequences are not."},{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 3: Amidst the Turmoil of Events, Do Not Lose Your Presence of Mind – The Counterbalance Strategy","idea":"Frustration (and the neighbour comparison) can push her into anger, which he can wait out. Calm and firm is harder to ignore."}],"sampleResponse":"I'd switch to writing (WhatsApp/email) and set terms: \\"Install was committed for 1 Oct. Please confirm a final install date by Friday. Remaining 50% will be released only on completed installation and inspection. If we don't have a confirmed date by Friday, I'll need to engage another vendor to complete the work and discuss refund of the uninstalled portion.\\" I'd keep the balance as leverage and never release it early. Visiting the workshop once, unannounced, also signals seriousness. If he still delays, follow through: honest, factual online reviews and, if needed, a consumer complaint. Each step escalates calmly. About the neighbour: I'd note it, not let it rattle me, and rely on my written terms.","commonMistake":"Making long angry calls, or releasing more money in the hope it speeds things up. The victim version: concluding \\"they never take women seriously\\" and giving up, rather than using the leverage she actually has."},{"id":"S25","title":"The Relative Who Belittles You at the Wedding","category":"Family and society","difficulty":"easy","scenario":"At a cousin's wedding in Vijayawada, an uncle says loudly in front of relatives: \\"Software job, okay, but what's the use? Look at Kiran, he's in the US earning in dollars. You're still in Hyderabad doing coding?\\" A few people laugh. Your mother looks uncomfortable.","question":"What would you do?","principles":[{"source":"The 33 Strategies of War","citation":"Strategy 26: Deny Them Targets – The Strategy of the Void","idea":"A provocation needs a reaction. Give him nothing to push against and it falls flat."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 10: Beware the Fragile Ego – The Law of Envy","idea":"Public put-downs often come from comparison and insecurity, sometimes his own. Seeing that takes the sting out."},{"source":"The 48 Laws of Power","citation":"Law 4: Always Say Less Than Necessary","idea":"A short, light reply comes across as more confident than a long defence of her career."},{"source":"The Laws of Human Nature","citation":"Ch. 1: Master Your Emotional Self – The Law of Irrationality","idea":"The heat is in the moment. Her composure is what the relatives will remember."}],"sampleResponse":"Smile, light tone: \\"Kiran's doing great. Hyderabad's doing pretty well for me too, uncle! Now, have you tried the pootharekulu?\\" and move on. No defending the salary, no comparisons, no counterattack. I've denied him a target. Later, I'd quietly reassure my mother if she seemed hurt: \\"Amma, I'm happy with where I'm going. That's what matters.\\" I'd also note that how I carry myself here is visible to everyone, and calm, good-humoured confidence is the strongest answer in a family setting.","commonMistake":"Either justifying herself with numbers and job titles (\\"Actually I earn ₹X and AI is...\\"), which gives the uncle the argument he wanted, or a sharp comeback that embarrasses her mother and becomes the story of the wedding. Carrying it home and brooding for days is the private version of the same mistake."}]`);export{e as default};